<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!--
    Which e_machine values map to the VideoCore VPU language.

    Without this file the ELF loader has no way to pick a language for a VideoCore object and the
    import fails outright with `LoadException: No load spec found`. Naming the language explicitly
    still works and is still supported as an override (`vpu-probe/decompile-fns.py` passed
    language="vc4:LE:32:default" for exactly this reason), but every caller had to know that, and
    the GUI import path had no escape hatch short of picking from the full language list by hand.

    137 (EM_VIDEOCORE3) is what every image in our corpus actually carries: start.elf, start4.elf
    from 2023 and 2026, both EEPROM bootmains, and all 35 VideoCore III .vll modules. Our own
    binutils emits it too (`ELF_MACHINE_CODE = EM_VIDEOCORE3` in bfd/elf32-vc4.c). Certain.

    Stronger than corpus agreement, the SILICON's own loader insists on it. bootmain's header
    validator at 0x8e9ae rejects any image whose e_machine is not 137, alongside ELFCLASS32,
    ELFDATA2LSB and ET_EXEC (docs/bcm2711-boot.md). So on this platform 137 is not merely the number
    the toolchains happen to emit: it is the only number the boot ROM will load.

    THE NUMERAL IS NOT A GENERATION MISMATCH, which is worth saying because it looks like one. The
    III / IV / V / VI / VII numerals track the V3D/QPU block, not the VPU: the VPU stays in the
    VideoCore IV lineage across all of them, and the hardware version word agrees (0x04000161
    measured on BCM2711, against stock's own whitelist of five revisions, every one 0x0400xxxx). So
    "a VC6 start4.elf" means "firmware for a part whose 3D block is VideoCore VI" and says nothing
    about its VPU. One machine number covering every VideoCore lineage object we have is therefore
    the expected outcome, not a defect in the numbering.

    Nothing found so far suggests that mapping is wrong. #78 went looking: the 35 VC3 plugins are
    MetaWare linked from a Symbian product line rather than Broadcom's Pi toolchain, and they
    disassemble against our VC4 spec at 0.43% undecoded over 316,097 instructions, the same order as
    the Pi images. Nineteen mnemonics occur there and nowhere in the Pi corpus and not one of them is
    vector; they are known scalar opcodes carrying unseen condition suffixes, plus `.case`.

    The limit worth stating next to that: it is CORPUS evidence, i.e. what two toolchains chose to
    emit, not SILICON evidence. The only silicon level ISA measurement in this project is #106's
    boundary (aluops 0..55 execute, 56..63 raise exception 0x03) and it was taken on BCM2711 alone.
    Repeating that sweep on another part is what would actually test ISA identity; see task #111.

    95 (EM_VIDEOCORE) is ELF_MACHINE_ALT1 in the same binutils backend, so the toolchain accepts it
    as an alternate and something out there presumably emits it. NOTHING IN OUR CORPUS DOES, so this
    entry is a reasonable guess rather than an observation. Recorded as such deliberately.

    198 (EM_VIDEOCORE5) IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and the omission is load bearing.
    Per docs/videocore-vpu-isa.md, "VideoCore V" most likely never existed as a VPU in silicon at
    all: two independent Broadcom authored BOLT device trees agree on the complete bus master roll
    and contain no VPU, with the `brcm,memc-client-masks` bitmaps matching the named clients exactly
    (134/134 and 112/112), so that is an exclusion and not an argument from silence; a 300 MB OpenPLi
    image for confirmed VideoCore V silicon (BCM7251S) holds 687 ARM ELF objects, one ARC, and zero
    VideoCore objects; and the clock and power role our VPU fills is done there by an ARC core.
    Number 198 was allocated to Broadcom by late 2011, roughly five years before "VideoCore V"
    shipped as a V3D generation that inherited the name without the core.

    So no object anywhere has ever reported 198. That is precisely why claiming it would be harmful
    rather than merely wrong: if a 198 object ever does surface it is a significant find, and an
    opinion entry here would have Ghidra quietly decode it as VC4 and destroy the signal that it is
    something new. `No load spec found` is the correct behaviour for a number nobody emits. If one
    turns up, docs/videocore-vpu-isa.md names vpu-probe/pi5-survey.py as the first instrument.
-->

<opinions>
    <constraint loader="Executable and Linking Format (ELF)" compilerSpecID="default">
        <!-- EM_VIDEOCORE3. Every VideoCore object in the corpus, and what our binutils emits. -->
        <constraint primary="137" processor="vc4" endian="little" size="32" variant="default" />
        <!-- EM_VIDEOCORE. binutils' ELF_MACHINE_ALT1; accepted, but unobserved in our corpus. -->
        <constraint primary="95"  processor="vc4" endian="little" size="32" variant="default" />
    </constraint>
</opinions>
